  "answer": "## Where the “existing option selection” behavior is implemented\n\nAutocomplete selection + keyboard/mouse “active option” handling lives in the shared hook: `packages/mui-base/src/useAutocomplete/useAutocomplete.js` (this repo’s `useAutocomplete` is the core engine that both Joy and Material wrappers use) (see retrieved file entry for `packages/mui-base/src/useAutocomplete/useAutocomplete.js` in the wiki excerpts).\n\nThe Joy wrapper (`packages/mui-joy/src/Autocomplete/Autocomplete.tsx`) wires the hook to the UI, including the option/listbox components and equality/labeling behavior. It provides defaults like `getOptionLabel` and also passes `isOptionEqualToValue` through to the hook (`packages/mui-joy/src/Autocomplete/Autocomplete.tsx`) (the excerpt shows the default helpers like `defaultGetOptionLabel` and the main prop destructuring, including `isOptionEqualToValue` and `getOptionLabel`).\n\n## Likely cause (based on what’s exposed in the wrapper)\n\nFor “existing option selection” bugs, the usual culprit is a mismatch between:\n- the *option identity* used to determine “this option is already selected” (`isOptionEqualToValue`), and/or\n- the *option label* / rendered option key used for active/selected styling.\n\nThe Joy wrapper explicitly supports `isOptionEqualToValue` and `getOptionLabel`:\n- `isOptionEqualToValue` is accepted as a prop in `packages/mui-joy/src/Autocomplete/Autocomplete.tsx` (in the big destructuring list shown in the excerpt).\n- `getOptionLabel` defaults to `option.label ?? option` (`defaultGetOptionLabel` in `packages/mui-joy/src/Autocomplete/Autocomplete.tsx`).\n\nSo a fix for “fixed autocomplete’s existing option selection” would typically ensure the hook receives stable/equivalent option/value comparisons and that the selected option remains “active” after reopening or after prop updates—both managed via `useAutocomplete` and the equality functions provided by the wrapper.\n\n## What to inspect next in code (to validate 